Hosting Rooms means setting up three separate things: the web address people
open, the room server their sessions join, and the routes two peers use to
talk directly. Decide each one on its own. HTTPS on the page does not make
every peer route reachable, and changing how direct messages travel does not
change how sessions are shared.

# Choose the shape

For local development, `zig build rooms-web` serves the page on your machine
and runs the room server over encrypted iroh in a temporary data directory.
`zig build rooms-dev` keeps its state between runs and turns on every
admin capability.

To host it, install the glowcap product and Docker with Compose, then run:

```sh
./glowcap apps rooms deployment up
```

That starts the page, an iroh room server, and one durable session service.
The image needs no checkout or compiler. The same command can check the stack,
show its logs, stop it, and reset one part of it after saying what will be
lost.

The example settings expose the page only on the host's loopback address. To
share it, put an HTTPS reverse proxy or a private network in front of it. That
proxy does not guard the room server, which people reach over iroh with its
own authentication. The
[deployment guide](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/apps/rooms/web/deploy/README.md) covers images, where state lives,
your own Compose settings, every environment variable, and the health checks.

# Choose how people reach it

The room server's transport decides how sessions reach it:

| Transport | Use it when |
| --- | --- |
| iroh | The default. Encrypted, the peer proves who it is, and it finds a path through most NATs. |
| onion | You want a Tor onion service. The service key lives in the data directory, so the address survives a restart. |
| TCP | You are developing on your own machine. It is unencrypted and proves nothing about who answered. |

The server prints an address you can paste only after it has opened its
listener and loaded its saved identity. If that identity cannot be used, or
another process already owns the data directory, the server stops instead
of quietly making a new one.

# Make sessions reachable

The web server carries bytes between a session on the page and the room
server over a WebSocket on the page's own address. The same relay lets another
peer reach that session. The encrypted connection is still between the two
peers.

Direct messages over WebRTC need ICE servers. STUN helps two peers find a
direct route, and TURN carries the traffic when a network will not allow
one. Rooms can read a fixed ICE configuration, or ask a TLS service for
short-lived TURN credentials without ever giving the page that service's
secret.

If two peers exchange an offer but no route can be found, switching direct
messages to the relay usually brings the conversation back. It does not
change **Share this session** or **Attach to another session**. A shared
session advertises its own routes, so trouble there is reported on its own.

# Run the session service

Without it, a session lives on one device. To open the same session, with
the same identity, from anywhere, run the session service:

```sh
glowcap apps rooms session serve --dir=.local/.state/rooms
```

The command prints a link to the session. Open it from the **Session** tab in Settings.
The service keeps the rooms, conversations, drafts, saved capabilities, and
history choices. Attaching does not copy the browser's previous session into
it. Every device that opens the link sees the same rooms, conversations, and
drafts.

Management is separate. Its link is kept in the service's state directory,
and the [operating guide](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/apps/rooms/operating.md#the-session-service) says where. Hold
that link in **Session access** to create access for a person or device,
change its permissions, pause it, or revoke it. The new link grants
only the use you select, and you can share a separately limited management
link. Reading history and reading files are separate permissions.

The session checks what a link may do each time it is used, so you can narrow
a link, add or remove earlier messages, or pause it while someone holds it,
without changing the link. A paused link says it is paused and why. Revoking
a link cuts off every open connection using it, and it stays revoked after a
restart.

# Keep the data directory together

Only one process may use a Rooms data directory at a time. Stop the server,
then copy or restore the directory as a whole. It holds the server's saved
state, the record of unfinished work, its identity, bridge settings, saved credentials, and kept room history. A
partial copy can pair old settings with a new identity, which the server cannot
repair.

The session and archive services have data directories of their own, with
the same rule: one process, copied as a whole, and stop the service before
you move it.

# Decide who keeps history

Three places can keep history:

- The server can keep the last lines of each room for people who arrive
  later.
- A session can keep room and direct-message history on the device it lives
  on.
- An archive service can keep messages and files beyond any device or
  server.

History on the server is capped for each room and for the whole server.
History in a session or an archive follows what each member chose. A shared archive link can be
read without letting the reader write to it or administer it.

A kept line counts as saved only once storage acknowledges it, and recovery
stops rather than guessing when the stored history is damaged.

# Hand out admin links

Diagnostics, listener administration, peering, IRC, the operator page, and
the live wire view each have their own secret, created when the server starts
with that feature on. The web address alone gives you none of them. Paste a
secret into the **Holdings** tab in Settings and the matching page appears under
`/ops`.

[The server page under /ops, opened with the admin secret. It lists what the admin can change: room links, registered names, live connections, and the references each one holds, with a remove or disconnect button beside every row.]($image.siteAsset('rooms-operations-dark.webp'))

[The server page under /ops, opened with the admin secret. It lists what the admin can change: room links, registered names, live connections, and the references each one holds, with a remove or disconnect button beside every row.]($image.siteAsset('rooms-operations-light.webp'))

The live wire view sends wire traffic to ocapnviz, glowcap's
protocol viewer. A server can show full room
traffic. For a person's direct messages it shows only who talked to whom,
when, which call, and how many bytes, because the other person never agreed to
let a viewer read them.

The full
[operating guide](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/apps/rooms/operating.md)
covers each flag, the saved files, the IRC forms, and
how to set up the live view.

# Read failures as they are

The deployment tells apart a service that refused to start, one that stopped
after it was ready, and a cleanup that also failed. Docker restarts services
according to its Compose policy, while `status` and `logs` show the current
state and reason.

Your session does the same. A request still in progress stays marked in
progress, a known refusal offers the next step, and a request nobody can
confirm is never shown as cleanly undone.
